<BaseLayout
  title="Cross-Consumer Authority Enforcement in a Persistent Agent System: A Bounded Case Study"
  description="Public HTML edition of research note v0.1.1: one canonical authority decision over one governed persistent state, enforced inconsistently across heterogeneous behavioral consumers in one audited agent-memory substrate."
>
  <div class="container-shell py-10">
    <div class="note-shell">

      <Breadcrumb path="/research/building-trustworthy-ai/authority-enforcement/note" />

      <p class="note-back-top">
        <a href={OVERVIEW_URL}>← Back to project overview</a>
      </p>

      <!-- ============ Title ============ -->
      <header class="note-header">
        <span class="scope-badge">Research note v0.1.1 · bounded single-substrate case study</span>
        <h1 class="note-title">Cross-Consumer Authority Enforcement in a Persistent Agent System: A Bounded Case Study</h1>
      </header>

      <!-- ============ Provenance banner (prominent, above the abstract) ============ -->
      <section class="note-prov" aria-label="Provenance and evidence banner">
        <p class="note-prov-lead">
          <strong>Provenance and evidence banner.</strong> This note is a synthesis and writing artifact. It reports <strong>no new experiments</strong>. All empirical content is inherited from frozen internal artifacts (v0.1–v0.4, the IJFW consumer-consistency audit, the independent adversarial review, the novelty/overlap audit, and the cross-substrate replication package). Consolidation recovered and hash-verified those records; an independent adversarial reviewer inspected the frozen artifacts. Neither consolidation, the adversarial review, the novelty audit, nor this note independently re-executed the experiments. Throughout this note, “supported”, “demonstrated”, “observed”, “reduced”, and “eliminated” refer to the <strong>recorded frozen evidence</strong>, not to a fresh replication.
        </p>
        <ul class="note-prov-labels">
          {provenance.map((p) => (
            <li class="note-prov-item">
              <span class="note-prov-label">{p.label}</span>
              <span class="note-prov-gloss">{p.gloss}</span>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
        <p class="note-prov-scope">
          <strong>Scope in one line:</strong> one audited substrate, one synthetic fixture, participating operations and cooperating writers, seven declared entry paths, deterministic check-time detection, raw/audit observation preserved on audited non-behavioral paths. Original low-level modules remain technically callable; <code class="note-code">bypass_possible = true</code> outside the mediated interface. “Canonical” means canonical within the frozen artifact set, not universally canonical for agent systems.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ Abstract ============ -->
      <section class="note-section" id="abstract">
        <h2 class="note-h2">Abstract</h2>
        <div class="note-prose">
          <p>
            Persistent agent state is frequently governed by an explicit authority decision — an approval, a revocation, a scope restriction — recorded separately from the state itself. This note reports a bounded case study of what happens <em>after</em> that decision exists. In one audited open-source agent-memory substrate (IJFW, release v1.6.5, pinned commit <code class="note-code">eda62f39…</code>), the same canonical authority decision over the same governed state was enforced inconsistently across heterogeneous behavioral consumers: the state was excluded by one context constructor and admitted by a second context constructor, with a real MCP tool response confirming that the admission reaches an emitted boundary — a further behaviorally relevant exposure path rather than independent evidence, because that response reuses the same brief path — while raw and audit visibility was preserved in every condition. We describe a four-stage constructive ladder built on that substrate: shared enforcement semantics across consumers (v0.1); completed-transition freshness under current-state lookup (v0.2); a deliberate falsification showing that current-state lookup is insufficient when authority changes between the check and the use, together with a sufficient atomic decision–use boundary (v0.3); and fixed-purpose mediated entry points with executable inventory checks that reduce undetected protocol omissions to zero within seven declared entry paths (v0.4). All results are bounded to one substrate, one synthetic fixture, participating operations and cooperating writers. The ladder measures construction and emission of behavioral context, not downstream model behavior. An attempted replication on a structurally qualified second substrate (Honcho) remained unresolved because its runtime dependency was unavailable after a single preregistered recovery attempt; no mechanism or behavioral inference was drawn from it. We claim no priority, no universal architecture, no repository-wide prevention, and no cross-system generalization.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ 1. Motivation and Scope ============ -->
      <section class="note-section" id="motivation-and-scope">
        <h2 class="note-h2">1. Motivation and Scope</h2>
        <div class="note-prose">
          <p>
            The broader research agenda behind this work is <strong>reliable adaptation under imperfect feedback</strong>: agent systems that learn from feedback, corrections, and observed outcomes must decide whether what they have learned should continue to govern behavior when the evidence, scope, or authorization conditions around it change. That agenda has several subprograms. This one is bounded to <strong>behavioral authority over persistent agent state</strong> — the question of whether a stored item that is <em>present</em> and <em>governed</em> is also <em>permitted</em> to enter the paths that shape agent behavior.
          </p>
          <p>Two distinctions organize everything that follows.</p>
          <p>
            <strong>State existence ≠ behavioral eligibility.</strong> A persistent store can hold an item — a correction, a preference, a derived conclusion, a commitment — while the system’s own authority rules say that the item must not be assembled into the context that drives the next action. Storage presence is a property of the store; behavioral eligibility is a property of the consumer. Confusing them turns every retained record into an authorization.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Observable ≠ behaviorally eligible.</strong> An authorized operator, an audit tool, or a debugging interface may legitimately <em>see</em> an item that no behavioral constructor may <em>use</em>. Visibility is an inspection capability. Counting it as an enforcement failure collapses two different predicates and makes every well-designed admin path look like a leak.
          </p>
          <p>
            This note is deliberately narrow. It does not argue that persistent-state authority is a new concept; that framing is already present in 2026 work (Section 7). It does not propose a general lifecycle, a universal enforcement architecture, or a revised authorization semantics. It reports one audited instance of a specific failure shape, a constructive ladder that addresses it on that substrate, and one deliberate falsification that narrowed the claim.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Scope assumptions, stated up front.</strong> One audited substrate (IJFW, release v1.6.5). One synthetic governed state reconstructed through the substrate’s real capture and derivation pipeline. Three behavioral consumer paths and two observational paths. Local filesystem concurrency with cooperating canonical writers. Seven declared entry adapters. Downstream model behavior, host adoption, and real-user deployment are <strong>not</strong> measured anywhere in this note.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ 2. Behavioral Authority and Consumer Enforcement ============ -->
      <section class="note-section" id="behavioral-authority">
        <h2 class="note-h2">2. Behavioral Authority and Consumer Enforcement</h2>
        <div class="note-prose">
          <p>
            Let <em>s</em> be a governed persistent state item. Let <strong>A(s)</strong> be the canonical authority decision for a given purpose — in the audited substrate, an entry in an out-of-band approval registry combined with a citation-locator predicate, evaluated fail-closed so that absent, unknown, pending, and rejected all resolve to <em>not approved</em>. Let <strong>C₁ … Cₙ</strong> be the <em>consumers</em>: code paths that read persistent state and either assemble behaviorally relevant context or emit a response. Each consumer resolves its own admission predicate, yielding <strong>Eᵢ(s)</strong>, the behavioral eligibility of <em>s</em> at <em>Cᵢ</em>. Let <strong>Oⱼ(s)</strong> be observability of <em>s</em> at an inspection path <em>j</em>.
          </p>
          <p>
            The failure shape studied here is <strong>consumer-enforcement divergence</strong>: <em>A(s)</em> is fixed and correct, yet <em>Eᵢ(s)</em> differs across <em>i</em>. Neither the store nor the decision is inconsistent; the inconsistency lives in how many independent admission predicates the system maintains for what its own declarations describe as one decision.
          </p>
          <p>Two structural observations make this shape easy to miss:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>Partial centralization is enough to look centralized.</strong> The audited substrate stores one authority decision in one place and evaluates it through one canonical predicate function. But only some consumers call that function. Reuse of adjacent filters (sensitivity, redaction, token budget) creates the appearance of shared enforcement without sharing the authority decision. The measured architecture label was therefore <code class="note-code">PARTIALLY_CENTRALIZED</code>, not centralized and not fully distributed.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>Divergence can be silent.</strong> If no consumer emits a diagnostic comparing its own admission outcome against the canonical decision, the system has no internal signal that one path disagrees with another. Divergence is then discoverable only by paired probing across consumers under a held-fixed state.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Note what this analysis does <strong>not</strong> claim. It does not evaluate whether <em>A(s)</em> itself was warranted — whether the approval should have been granted, or whether a retained approval outlived its justifying evidence. Those are upstream questions about evidence quality, attribution, and maintenance. They are separable, and separating them is what lets a consumer-enforcement finding be stated without also claiming an authorization failure.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ 3. Case Study ============ -->
      <section class="note-section" id="case-study">
        <h2 class="note-h2">3. Case Study: Cross-Consumer Enforcement Divergence</h2>
        <div class="note-prose">
          <p>
            <strong>Substrate.</strong> IJFW is an open-source JavaScript agent-profile system (MCP server plus CLI and host hooks) that derives a persistent user profile from captured editing sessions, stores approval state out-of-band, and serves that profile through several output paths. Audit pin: commit <code class="note-code">eda62f39f08b3028d824de1d806fe05c1402fd94</code>, release v1.6.5, clean <code class="note-code">main</code> checkout. Source was read-only throughout.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Governed state.</strong> One synthetic correction atom recovered through the substrate’s real capture, derivation, and merge pipeline: <code class="note-code">correction::javascript::keep every parser error message beside the raise site in the same mo</code>, with three non-adjacent synthetic sessions supplying capture records and matching feedback. The complete profile was byte-identical across all conditions (SHA256 <code class="note-code">4da8041121f8…</code>). The authority carrier is <code class="note-code">approvals.json</code>, separate from the profile; the canonical admission predicate requires an approved state <em>and</em> a real citation locator. Host, sharing configuration, sensitivity policy, and token budget were held fixed.
          </p>
          <p><strong>Consumers.</strong> Three behavioral paths were exercised at the pin:</p>
        </div>

        <NoteTable
          caption="Table — behavioral consumer paths exercised at the pin"
          columns={['Path', 'Role', 'Measured effect']}
          rows={consumersRows}
        />

        <div class="note-prose mt-6">
          <p>
            Two additional paths were exercised as controls: <code class="note-code">profileGet</code> (raw structured retrieval, RETRIEVABLE) and <code class="note-code">listInferences</code> / <code class="note-code">profile.audit</code> (inspection and provenance listing, OBSERVABLE_ONLY). MCP delegates to the brief path, so the three behavioral outputs are not three independent authority sources — the divergence of interest is between the <em>snapshot</em> branch and the <em>brief</em> branch, with the MCP row confirming that the divergence reaches a real emitted response.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Conditions and result.</strong> Four authority conditions — absent, pending, approved, rejected — were run in two separate fresh Node processes for deterministic mechanism reproduction — not independent experimental replication, not independent systems, and not model-level evidence — with 15 observations per condition per process (120 total), and with profile bytes and approval-registry bytes held fixed within each condition.
          </p>
        </div>

        <NoteTable
          caption="Table — authority condition × consumer outcome"
          columns={['Authority condition', 'Snapshot context', 'Brief context', 'MCP brief response', 'Raw / audit visibility', 'Classification']}
          rows={conditionsRows}
        />

        <div class="note-prose mt-6">
          <p>
            The absent/approved pair is the minimum decisive intervention; pending and rejected confirm that the omission is not an empty-registry special case. Encoded as a vector over the three behavioral consumers under the unapproved condition, the observed baseline is <code class="note-code">[false, true, true]</code> — <strong>two disagreeing consumer pairs</strong> — and the approved control is <code class="note-code">[true, true, true]</code> with zero false blocks.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What this is not.</strong> Raw <code class="note-code">profile.get</code> returning the unapproved atom, and <code class="note-code">profile.audit</code> listing it, are <em>not</em> counted as violations. Both are inspection features operating under their own visibility, sensitivity, and redaction rules; their rows deliberately carry no behavioral-eligibility value. The finding is also not a claim about model behavior. The audit measured <em>context eligibility</em> — whether a governed item is assembled into the text and fields that a behavioral constructor emits, and whether that text actually reaches an MCP response boundary. No LLM was invoked, no host prompt was observed, no ranking or action was measured. The verdict at the pin was <code class="note-code">CONSUMER_INCONSISTENCY_CONFIRMED</code>, bounded to these consumers and this opt-in configuration.
          </p>
        </div>

        <figure class="note-figure">
          <FigureACrossConsumerDivergence />
          <figcaption class="note-figcaption">
            Figure 1 — one canonical authority state feeding heterogeneous consumers with divergent behavioral eligibility, with the observational arm shown separately as intentionally non-behavioral.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ 4. Constructive Enforcement Ladder ============ -->
      <section class="note-section" id="enforcement-ladder">
        <h2 class="note-h2">4. Constructive Enforcement Ladder</h2>
        <div class="note-prose">
          <p>
            The four stages below are paired, preregistered experiments on the same pinned substrate. Each stage begins from a boundary left open by the previous one. Classifications were frozen before execution in every stage, and failed or repaired attempts were retained rather than recast.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="note-h3">4.1 Shared enforcement (v0.1) — spatial consumer consistency</h3>
        <div class="note-prose">
          <p>
            <strong>Question.</strong> If all behavioral consumers resolve eligibility through the <em>same</em> canonical decision semantics, does the observed cross-consumer divergence disappear — and does it disappear without destroying legitimate observation?
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Intervention.</strong> A three-file patch extending the existing decision seam: the existing approval-plus-citation predicate function was retained unchanged, the canonical registry was threaded into the brief path, and only preference/correction atoms in the brief’s behavioral branch were filtered. No authority API, authority state, derivation logic, atom content, or predicate semantics changed. The MCP dispatcher needed no separate gate because its response is produced by the brief path.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Result.</strong> Paired 8-cell matrix, baseline versus post, with the profile SHA256 identical across arms. Unapproved pairwise consumer inconsistencies <strong>2 → 0</strong>; approved false blocks <strong>0</strong>; lost legitimate audit visibility <strong>0</strong>. The same invariant, run against the frozen pre-intervention vector <code class="note-code">[false, true, true]</code>, fails — so the metric discriminates rather than merely passing. A deterministic regression test reconstructed the fixture and exercised a real MCP initialization and tool call: 1 passed, 0 failed.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Reading.</strong> A centralized decision store existed before v0.1 and was insufficient. What changed was that each behavioral path began <em>consuming</em> the shared decision. The supported claim is about shared consumption of one decision semantics, not about centralizing storage.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="note-h3">4.2 Transition freshness (v0.2) — completed grant/revoke transitions</h3>
        <div class="note-prose">
          <p>
            <strong>Question.</strong> v0.1 was static. If a canonical grant or revocation <em>completes</em>, do new constructions and requests observe the new authority without restarting the process?
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Intervention. None.</strong> This stage changed zero implementation bytes; its “post” arm is a second execution of the identical frozen matrix against the recovered pinned source. It is declared replication, not treatment.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Result.</strong> Six retained groups plus two fresh-control groups yield 32 rows per execution (24 behavioral, 8 inspection). Across two independent executions: stale allows after revocation <strong>0</strong>, stale blocks after grant <strong>0</strong>, approved false blocks <strong>0</strong>, revoked false allows <strong>0</strong>, lost audit visibility <strong>0</strong>. Retained direct and MCP processes kept stable PIDs across both transitions; fresh-control processes in each arm observed the same current authority. The primary evidence is the real per-invocation canonical read traces together with retained/fresh agreement. A deliberately stale supplied-registry fixture produces 2 stale blocks and 2 stale allows per run and is rejected by the same classifier — this is a <strong>sensitivity fixture for detector calibration</strong>, not the primary evidence for freshness, and no freshness classification depends on it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Reading.</strong> <code class="note-code">CURRENT_STATE_LOOKUP_SUFFICIENT</code> holds in the tested serialized regime: for operations that <em>begin after</em> a completed transition, per-invocation canonical lookup sufficed, and no authority version, transition timestamp comparison, invalidation channel, or content version was needed. This is deliberately not a concurrency result. v0.2 never claimed decision–use atomicity, and Section 4.3 shows that the stronger reading is false.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="note-h3">4.3 Decision–use atomicity (v0.3) — falsification of current-state lookup</h3>
        <div class="note-prose">
          <p><strong>Question.</strong> Is fresh lookup at check time sufficient when authority changes <em>between</em> the check and the use?</p>
          <p>This is the stage where the ladder deliberately tries to break its own prior result, and it succeeds.</p>
          <p>
            <strong>Exposed failure.</strong> The three behavioral paths are request-local: each resolves the registry into a local set or map, checks membership, and then builds the target line, inserts the field, or — for MCP — serializes and writes the response. No post-check revalidation and no consumer-side lock connects that local decision to a concurrently executing canonical writer. A harness that pauses after the native membership check has computed its value, while an independent worker completes a canonical transition, and then resumes the original consumer, produces on unchanged source: <strong>3 stale-allow races</strong> (approved check, completed revocation, stale admission) and <strong>3 stale-block races</strong> (pending check, completed grant, stale exclusion), across all three consumers. Ordering was established with shared-memory barriers and real writer schedules, not sleeps or probabilistic timing.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Repair.</strong> A 14-line helper encloses the supplied operation in the substrate’s <strong>existing</strong> profile lock, spanning fresh lookup through selected text/field construction, or through actual MCP emission and receipt. No new lock protocol, authority version, timestamp comparison, or content version was introduced. In the post matrix, all six attempted inside-window writes hit real contention on the held canonical lock and completed only after construction or emission; stale allows and stale blocks both go to <strong>0</strong>, with 0/36 lost individual raw/audit visibility checks.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Reading.</strong> The scientific result is the race and the placement of the boundary: correctness requires temporal coherence between the authority decision and the selected use. The shared profile lock is <strong>one sufficient implementation</strong> on this substrate for participating operations with cooperating writers; its universal necessity is not claimed, and equivalent revalidation, versioning, or transactional semantics remain conceptually available and untested here. The guarantee ends at the measured construction/emission boundary — it does not extend to arbitrary later use of returned data, to unwrapped callers, to non-cooperating writes, or to lock failure and recovery.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h3 class="note-h3">4.4 Mediated entry and bypass detection (v0.4) — protocol participation</h3>
        <div class="note-prose">
          <p>
            <strong>Question.</strong> v0.3’s boundary was opt-in: correctness still depended on each caller <em>remembering</em> to participate. Can participation be selected and verified mechanically?
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Intervention.</strong> Two new native modules — a fixed-purpose access facade and a private persistent MCP transport — plus seven rewritten isolated entry adapters and a closed AST/import/hash gate. All 291 recovered original implementation files remain byte-identical (traceable to <code class="note-code">authority_protocol_completeness_v01/AUTHORITY_PROTOCOL_COMPLETENESS_V01_REPORT.md</code>, which records “all 291 recovered source hashes unchanged”; the same figure is restated in the independent artifact review). The facade separates three purposes: observational access (raw, audit), qualified behavioral construction (snapshot, brief, MCP brief), and canonical mutation (grant, revoke). Caller lock overrides inconsistent with the canonical lock are refused. The gate resolves aliases, checks exact module paths and literal purpose selectors, checks the trusted role inventory and implementation fingerprints, and rejects extra, computed, or dynamic source as UNRESOLVED rather than certifying it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Result.</strong> Declared-adapter complete-protocol omissions <strong>3 → 0</strong> within the seven frozen declared entry paths; writer bypasses <strong>0 → 0</strong> (canonical writers were already correctly locked, so no writer defect is fabricated); legitimate raw observers <strong>2 → 2</strong>; fully mediated behavioral/writer paths 2 → 5. The gate exits 0 on the normal inventory and 1 with exact evidence on four violating/unresolved fixtures. Across eleven bounded cases — including a raw behavioral reader that actually formats pending content, a direct filesystem writer that actually flips approvals outside the API and lock, observer-result repurposing, legacy and aliased access, and counterfeit paths — eight are DETECTED, two are REFUSED_AT_CHECK_TIME_BY_THE_GATE (fixture-scoped gate refusal; no structural prevention implied), one correct-alias case NOT_APPLICABLE, and none is silently allowed within the frozen inventory. Seven entry runtime outputs are exactly equal before and after mediation.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Reading — and the ceiling of the claim.</strong> This is <strong>check-time deterministic detection over a declared inventory</strong>, not structural prevention. The original low-level modules remain technically callable; undeclared programs, caller intent outside trusted roles, a disabled or replaced gate, non-cooperating writers, and later reuse of already-returned values are outside scope. <code class="note-code">bypass_possible = true</code> remains true outside the controlled mediated interface. Stating this plainly is part of the result, not a hedge around it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <figure class="note-figure">
          <FigureBEnforcementLadder />
          <figcaption class="note-figcaption">
            Figure 2 — the v0.1 → v0.4 falsification ladder, showing each stage’s open boundary, the failure it exposed, and the guarantee it did and did not establish.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h3 class="note-h3">4.5 Ladder summary</h3>
        <p class="note-table-lead">TABLE 1 — v0.1–v0.4 constructive ladder.</p>
        <NoteTable
          caption="TABLE 1 — v0.1–v0.4 constructive ladder"
