      </section>

      <!-- ============ 5. What the Evidence Supports ============ -->
      <section class="note-section" id="what-the-evidence-supports">
        <h2 class="note-h2">5. What the Evidence Supports</h2>
        <div class="note-prose">
          <p>The strongest integrated statement supported by the four stages is:</p>
        </div>
        <blockquote class="note-quote">
          <p>
            <strong>On one audited persistent-agent substrate, the same canonical authority decision was enforced inconsistently across heterogeneous behavioral consumers. A shared enforcement contract removed that inconsistency while preserving approved behavior and legitimate raw/audit observability; subsequent falsification showed that completed-transition freshness was insufficient under decision–use races, motivating an atomic decision–use boundary and mediated access checks.</strong>
          </p>
        </blockquote>
        <div class="note-prose">
          <p>
            Each clause carries a separate warrant: “inconsistently” from the v0.1 paired matrix and the pre-existing audit finding; “shared enforcement contract” from the v0.1 repair that changed no authority predicate; “preserving legitimate observability” from zero lost visibility counts across all four stages on frozen non-behavioral paths; “falsification” from the v0.3 race on unchanged source; “atomic boundary” from the v0.3 post matrix with real writer contention; “mediated access checks” from the v0.4 executed negatives and gate. Removing those qualifiers would outrun the evidence.
          </p>
          <p>
            What the ladder establishes is a <strong>bounded enforcement contract spanning four regimes</strong> — spatial consumer consistency, completed-transition freshness, decision–use temporal integrity, and mediated protocol participation — under explicit scope assumptions. It is not a universal architecture. The mechanisms are standard (shared predicate, per-invocation lookup, one lock, a facade, an AST-level inventory check), the substrate is one synthetic fixture, and no alternative mechanism was experimentally compared. What is non-standard here is not any single mechanism but the shape of the evidence: a pre-existing cross-consumer divergence, a deliberate falsification that narrowed a prior positive result, and a participation check that is executable rather than asserted.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p class="note-table-lead">TABLE 2 — Claim boundary.</p>
        <NoteTable
          caption="TABLE 2 — Claim boundary"
          columns={['Claim', 'Status', 'Evidence', 'Scope', 'Limitation']}
          rows={boundaryRows}
        />

        <div class="note-prose mt-6">
          <p>
            The independent adversarial review of the frozen v0.4 consolidation recorded <code class="note-code">NO_FATAL_FLAW</code>, <code class="note-code">BOUNDED_REUSABLE_DESIGN_MECHANISM_CONFIRMED</code>, <code class="note-code">DETECTION_DISCIPLINE_PRESERVED</code>, <code class="note-code">TEMPORAL_CLAIM_SOUND</code>, <code class="note-code">SUBSTRATE_SCOPE_DISCIPLINED</code>, <code class="note-code">REGRESSION_CHAIN_INTACT</code>, <code class="note-code">PREREGISTRATION_DISCIPLINE_STRONG</code>, and <code class="note-code">EVIDENCE_MATURITY = READY_FOR_EXTERNAL_TECHNICAL_REVIEW</code>, with revision limited to <code class="note-code">MINOR_CLAIM_CORRECTION</code> and the original core result retained. That review inspected artifacts; it did not replicate them.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ 6. Cross-Substrate Replication Attempt ============ -->
      <section class="note-section" id="cross-substrate-replication">
        <h2 class="note-h2">6. Cross-Substrate Replication Attempt</h2>
        <div class="note-prose">
          <p>
            A single-substrate finding invites one obvious question: is the divergence a property of this codebase or a recurring property of persistent-agent substrates? One bounded replication attempt was preregistered and executed. It did not resolve.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Second substrate.</strong> Honcho (pinned commit <code class="note-code">8e4df990…</code>, version 3.2.2) was structurally qualified against six preregistered criteria before any mechanism execution. The selected native decision is a session recall allowlist governing whether a persistent peer card is eligible for scoped behavioral context — a recall boundary, not an API authorization or a human approval. Two independently implemented behavioral consumers were identified: (A) the native session-context constructor, and (B) the native peer-chat preflight path. An admin SQL read of the same stored card served as the observational control. All six criteria passed. Qualification establishes <em>eligibility to test</em>, not a result.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>What happened.</strong> Both fresh mechanism runs stopped before fixture creation: the required PostgreSQL dependency was unavailable. Syntax and import checks passed; zero contrast cells were produced. No model call, embedding call, transport canary, or behavioral pair was created, and IJFW was not rerun. One preregistered infrastructure recovery cycle was then permitted. Root cause was recorded before the action: the Docker backend could not initialize its global inference socket, leaving no listener on the declared database port; a normal Docker startup was attempted once, the same initialization failure recurred, and both database readiness probes timed out. No destructive global repair was performed. The recovery changed only transient processes and ordinary logs, and nothing in the frozen experiment design.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Final state.</strong> <code class="note-code">SECOND_SUBSTRATE_STATUS = QUALIFIED_HONCHO</code>; <code class="note-code">MECHANISM_STATUS = MECHANISM_PARTIAL</code>; <code class="note-code">BEHAVIORAL_STATUS = NOT_RUN</code>; <code class="note-code">FINAL_EXPERIMENT_STATUS = MECHANISM_UNRESOLVED</code>; <code class="note-code">NOVELTY_IMPACT = DIFFERENTIATION_UNCHANGED</code>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>How this should be read.</strong> Cross-substrate replication <strong>remained unresolved</strong>. It did not fail as a scientific result, and it did not succeed; an infrastructure failure is neither a replicated divergence nor a null. No mechanism or behavioral inference was drawn from Honcho, and the frozen single-substrate classifications were neither strengthened nor weakened. We report it here because a note that presents only its positive ladder would be hiding the one boundary that most constrains its own generality. Pre-registration, a single permitted recovery, and an explicit unresolved verdict are the discipline that makes the rest of the note readable.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ 7. Relation to Adjacent 2026 Work ============ -->
      <section class="note-section" id="adjacent-2026-work">
        <h2 class="note-h2">7. Relation to Adjacent 2026 Work</h2>
        <div class="note-prose">
          <p>
            All work discussed here precedes this project’s artifacts. No priority is claimed, and none is available on dates. The purpose of this section is to state what adjacent work already covers, what this note does not claim, and the narrow residue that remains.
          </p>
        </div>

        <NoteTable
          caption="Table — adjacent 2026 work and what this note does not claim against it"
          columns={['Work', 'What it already covers', 'What this note does not claim against it']}
          rows={adjacentRows}
        />

        <div class="note-prose mt-6">
          <p>
            The honest overlap assessment, from the completed novelty/overlap audit, is that the broad framings — persistent state as an authority object; staged authority failure; observable versus eligible; current lookup failing under a decision–use gap; atomic or revalidated boundaries as the remedy — are <strong>substantially to directly occupied</strong>. This note therefore does not present them as contributions.
          </p>
          <p>
            The narrow residue is <strong>cross-consumer enforcement divergence</strong>: one canonical authority decision resolved through several heterogeneous behavioral consumer code paths inside a single system yielding inconsistent behavioral eligibility while raw/audit observation is preserved. Within the targeted 2026 overlap audit, we did not identify a directly matching treatment of multiple behavioral consumer code paths resolving the same canonical authority decision; the adjacent work examined there studies <em>role</em>-level divergence (store versus model; writer versus executor) and <em>boundary</em>-level divergence (which commit condition breaks under which hazard). This is a search-bounded differentiation, not a claim that such work is absent from the broader literature; accordingly we treat cross-consumer code-path divergence as a narrow observed differentiation rather than a priority claim. Two things cap that residue: the evidence is one synthetic fixture with no downstream behavioral measurement, and the general notion of divergent policy enforcement points is long-established in security systems — so this is a new <em>instance in agent memory</em>, not a new concept. A secondary, weaker residue is the bidirectional staleness (stale-allow <em>and</em> stale-block) measured at the agent’s own context-construction boundary, upstream of durable commit.
          </p>
          <p>
            Accordingly, the audit’s external-form verdict is <code class="note-code">RESEARCH_NOTE_SEED</code>, with novelty <code class="note-code">DISTINCT_BUT_NARROW</code> and <code class="note-code">MIXED_RESEARCH_AND_HARDENING</code>. No strong differentiator was established, because no second substrate was successfully evaluated. This note is written to that ceiling.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ 8. Limitations ============ -->
      <section class="note-section" id="limitations">
        <h2 class="note-h2">8. Limitations</h2>
        <div class="note-prose">
          <p>Every item below is a real boundary of the evidence, not a rhetorical hedge.</p>
          <ol class="note-ol">
            <li><strong>One successfully evaluated substrate.</strong> All positive and negative results come from IJFW at one pinned release on one synthetic governed state. Nothing here establishes prevalence or generality.</li>
            <li><strong>No established cross-substrate replication.</strong> The one second substrate was structurally qualified but never executed; replication remains unresolved (Section 6).</li>
            <li><strong>No downstream model-behavior result.</strong> The ladder measures whether governed state is assembled into behavioral context and emitted at a response boundary. It does not measure model adoption, output change, ranking, host behavior, or actions.</li>
            <li><strong>No cross-model generalization.</strong> No model was called at any stage, and the separate behavioral pilot evidence in the wider program is single-model and unqualified for a second model.</li>
            <li><strong>No repository-wide bypass prevention.</strong> Detection covers the seven declared entry paths under a frozen trusted inventory. Original low-level modules remain technically callable and <code class="note-code">bypass_possible = true</code> remains true outside the mediated interface.</li>
            <li><strong>Non-cooperating writers remain outside the guarantee.</strong> The atomic boundary protects participating readers only against writers that use the same lock; direct storage writes are detected when present in an audited entry, not prevented.</li>
            <li><strong>No distributed-system guarantee.</strong> Tested concurrency is local: a real MCP child process and local canonical workers. Arbitrary processes, distributed stores and transactions, and network failure conditions were not tested.</li>
            <li><strong>No upstream authority-warrant guarantee.</strong> Whether an approval <em>should</em> have been granted, whether it was correctly attributed, and whether it survives changed evidence are separate questions this ladder does not address. A consumer can faithfully enforce an incorrect decision.</li>
            <li><strong>Much of the surrounding space is already occupied.</strong> Authority lifecycle, revocation, provenance, and commit-time authorization are each covered by prior 2026 work, several on far stronger substrates. This note claims no priority and no framework.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Additional standing boundaries: lock failure and recovery, nested writes under a non-reentrant lock, long-held operations, production deployment, retroactive revocation of already-delivered context, and removal of prior behavioral influence are all untested and unclaimed.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ 9. Discussion ============ -->
      <section class="note-section" id="discussion">
        <h2 class="note-h2">9. Discussion</h2>
        <div class="note-prose">
          <p>
            Why write this as a note rather than as a systems paper? Because the object is a bounded instance plus a falsification sequence, and its value depends on the reader being able to see exactly where the guarantees stop. A paper-shaped artifact would be pressured to promote the four-stage ladder into an architecture; the evidence does not support that, and the independent review specifically warned against letting the mechanism names travel without their scope qualifiers.
          </p>
          <p>Three aspects deserve emphasis.</p>
          <p>
            <strong>The negative result is the most informative step.</strong> v0.2 established that current-state lookup sufficed for completed transitions. v0.3 showed that the same mechanism fails under a decision–use race — 3 stale allows and 3 stale blocks on unchanged source, in both directions. A ladder that had stopped at v0.2 would have reported a clean positive result with a hidden boundary. The value of v0.3 is not that it found a lock useful; it is that it located the boundary where coherence between decision and use becomes necessary, and demonstrated that the substrate’s existing serialization primitive is one sufficient construction of it.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Detection is a legitimate engineering ceiling, and it should be stated as one.</strong> v0.4 reduces undetected protocol omissions to zero within seven declared paths. It does not make bypass impossible, because the underlying language and filesystem allow direct access and the original modules remain callable. Presenting that as prevention would be false and would be caught immediately by a reviewer who reads the inventory definition.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Discipline is part of the contribution, but not the whole of it.</strong> Preregistration before execution, frozen classifications before results, retained failed attempts, a 14-item non-guarantee list, and an independent adversarial review with no fatal flaws are methodological assets. On their own they would be worth little; the real observed cross-consumer divergence and the real bidirectional race are what they attach to. Conversely, those two findings without the discipline would not survive hostile reading. The note needs both.
          </p>
          <p>
            What would change the judgment is narrow and specific: a cross-substrate replication showing the same consumer-divergence shape in two or more independent agent-memory systems, with a downstream model-level consequence attached. That would move the surviving differentiator from moderate to strong. Its absence is currently the single largest constraint on this work, and Section 6 is the honest record of that absence.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ 10. Conclusion ============ -->
      <section class="note-section" id="conclusion">
        <h2 class="note-h2">10. Conclusion</h2>
        <div class="note-prose">
          <p>
            Persistent agent state can be governed by a clear canonical authority decision and still be enforced inconsistently, because authority is applied by consumers rather than by the store. On one audited substrate, one decision and one governed state produced divergent behavioral eligibility across heterogeneous consumers while raw and audit visibility was preserved. A shared enforcement contract removed that divergence; a completed-transition regime held under fresh lookup; a deliberate falsification showed fresh lookup was insufficient under decision–use races and that an atomic ordering boundary was sufficient for participating operations; and fixed-purpose mediated entry with executable inventory checks removed undetected protocol omissions within the declared paths. Each of those guarantees is bounded, and the note states where each one ends. An attempted second-substrate replication remained unresolved, and no inference was drawn from it. The result is a bounded enforcement contract and a documented falsification sequence on one substrate — not an architecture, not a lifecycle, and not a claim of priority.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ============ 11. Provenance / Evidence Note ============ -->
      <section class="note-section" id="provenance">
        <h2 class="note-h2">11. Provenance / Evidence Note</h2>
        <div class="note-prose">
          <p>Empirical content in this note is inherited from frozen artifacts and must be read with the following distinctions.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <strong>EMPIRICAL_EVIDENCE_FROM_FROZEN_RUNS.</strong> The v0.1–v0.4 results, the consumer-consistency audit, and the cross-substrate attempts were executed as preregistered experiments at the time they were created. This note reran none of them.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>RECOVERED_AND_HASH_VERIFIED_DURING_CONSOLIDATION.</strong> The consolidation step recovered the protected inputs and verified their presence, inventories, and hashes. Recovery verification is not re-execution.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>INDEPENDENT_ARTIFACT_REVIEWED.</strong> An independent adversarial reviewer (a different agent and model) inspected the frozen artifacts and produced the review and revision map cited in Section 5. That review inspected artifacts; it produced no separate empirical replication.
            </li>
            <li>
              <strong>NOT_INDEPENDENTLY_EMPIRICALLY_REPLICATED.</strong> No stage of this ladder has been reproduced by an independent agent or on an independent substrate. The single attempt at the latter remained unresolved.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            No frozen artifact was modified by this note. No experiment was run. No literature review beyond the completed novelty/overlap audit was performed, and no venue is proposed.
          </p>
          <p>
            Artifact locators, per-section source mapping, the full external claim ledger, and the correction log accompany this note in <code class="note-code">AUTHORITY_ENFORCEMENT_EXTERNAL_NOTE_V011_SOURCE_MAP.md</code>, <code class="note-code">AUTHORITY_ENFORCEMENT_EXTERNAL_NOTE_V011_CLAIM_LEDGER.csv</code>, and <code class="note-code">AUTHORITY_ENFORCEMENT_EXTERNAL_NOTE_V011_CORRECTION_LOG.md</code>. Figure specifications are unchanged from v0.1 and remain in <code class="note-code">AUTHORITY_ENFORCEMENT_EXTERNAL_NOTE_V011_FIGURE_SPECS.md</code>.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>CORRECTION_LAYER (v0.1.1).</strong> This document is the frozen v0.1 note with exactly six claim corrections (C1–C6) applied from the independent hostile review; see the correction log. No empirical claim, count, classification, or guarantee was changed, added, or removed by this layer.
          </p>
          <p class="note-status">
            End of Research Note v0.1.1. Status: the six corrections (C1–C6) from the independent hostile review have been applied. Suitable for PI/collaborator sharing, portfolio source material, and public technical circulation as a bounded single-substrate case study; not a submission-style manuscript seed, and no venue is proposed. No automatic revision follows.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

